Now I see the full picture. In `append_elements`:
1. `_gnutls_x509_read_value` reads the raw value into `tvd`
2. `_gnutls_x509_dn_to_string` converts it to a string in `td`
3. `DATA_APPEND(td.data, td.size)` appends the string to the buffer

The crash is in `decode_complex_string` which is called from `_gnutls_x509_dn_to_string`.

Let me look at the `_gnutls_x509_dn_to_string` function again:

```c
if (oentry->asn_desc != NULL) { /* complex */
    ret = decode_complex_string(oentry, value, value_size, &tmp);
    if (ret < 0) {
        goto unknown_oid;
    }
} else {
    ret = _gnutls_x509_decode_string(oentry->etype, value,
                                     value_size, &tmp, 0);
    if (ret < 0) {
        goto unknown_oid;
    }
}

ret = str_escape(&tmp, str);
_gnutls_free_datum(&tmp);
```

When `decode_complex_string` succeeds, `tmp` is properly set. Then `str_escape` is called with `&tmp`.

But wait - what if `decode_complex_string` succeeds but `tmp` has a NUL byte in the middle? Then `str_escape` would detect it and return an error. But that's not an uninitialized value issue.

Let me look at `str_escape` one more time:

```c
for (i = j = 0; i < str->size; i++) {
    if (str->data[i] == 0) {
        ret = gnutls_assert_val(GNUTLS_E_ASN1_DER_ERROR);
        goto cleanup;
    }
    ...
    buffer[j++] = str->data[i];
}
```

If `str->data[i]` is 0 (NUL), it returns an error. But this is a read of `str->data[i]`, which is initialized (it's part of the allocated buffer).

Wait, but what if `str->size` is uninitialized? Then the loop would iterate an unpredictable number of times, reading uninitialized memory. But `str` is `&tmp`, which is initialized to `{NULL, 0}` at the top of `_gnutls_x509_dn_to_string`. And `decode_complex_string` sets `tmp` properly on success.

